{"author":{"id":"694fb8161bd8bf91df2a28db7c649e48f8c5fa47ad3ad0234607d35f0482801a"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789210731,"nonce":"swars+6z3zOHAZmOldkzOHyzo2Y=","title":"hatch test: with_extension stripped .spec so import ./assert.spec loaded assert.wren","message":"Fixed; see git log for the landing commit. Record migrated from session memory 2026-09-12.\n\n`hatch test --filter \"\u003csub\u003e\"` / `--json` now work end-to-end. Resolved 2026-05-03.\n\n\nThe wlift CLI's relative-import resolver used `Path::with_extension(\"wren\")` to canonicalise `./foo` → `./foo.wren`. That call **replaces** the trailing extension instead of appending — so `import \"./assert.spec\"` got rewritten to `assert.wren` and silently loaded a same-stem sibling instead of the intended spec file. The wrapper-import path looked broken because the spec's top-level was never running; a different file was.\n\nFix lives in `with_wren_suffix(PathBuf) -\u003e PathBuf` in `src/main.rs`: returns the path unchanged if it already ends in `.wren`, otherwise appends `.wren` to the OS string. Four regression tests pin the no-extension, `.spec`, idempotent-`.wren`, and multi-dot cases.\n\n\nHatch CLI's `cmd_test` writes a per-spec runner file in the spec's parent directory (so the spec's own `./` imports resolve unchanged), prepends:\n\n```wren\nTest.filter = \"\u003cfilter\u003e\"\nTest.reporter = \"json\"\n```\n\n…then inlines the spec source verbatim. The spec's own `import \"@hatch:test\" for Test` registers `Test` during the import-processing pass, and the prepended assignment runs in source order during the body pass — well before the spec's trailing `Test.run()`. `tempfile::NamedTempFile` cleans up on drop.\n\n\n- \"**Per-module static slots**\" — false. `ObjClass.static_fields: HashMap\u003cSymbolId, Value\u003e` is class-keyed; static fields ARE shared across modules.\n- \"**Meta.eval doesn't share scope**\" — true but irrelevant. Meta.eval registers under `__eval_\u003ccalling-module\u003e__`, but static fields would still have been visible. The actual blocker was upstream of Meta entirely.\n- \"**Bare `import \"./X\"` doesn't run X's top-level**\" — false. It does. The `class A {}` + `System.print(\"hi\")` test in `/tmp` proved it. The hatch-assert test failed because the canonicalised path pointed at the wrong file.\n\n\n- `@hatch:test` 0.2.0 in the hatch repo: `Test.filter=` / `Test.reporter=` static setters, JSON-Lines reporter (start / pass / fail / skip / summary events with `duration_ms`), text reporter with per-case `Ok:` / `Fail:` lines + ✓/✗ glyph + total elapsed at the bottom, all colourised through `@hatch:fmt`. Self-spec extended.\n- `hatch test --filter \u003csubstr\u003e` and `hatch test --json` flags in the wren_lift `hatch` CLI. JSON mode streams events through to stdout for editor consumers; text mode keeps the existing colourised output.\n- `hatch test`'s `ok:` line parser made ANSI-tolerant + tolerant of the `(Xms)` timing suffix (regression caused by 0.2.0's colourised summary).\n- `with_wren_suffix` resolver helper in `src/main.rs` — the actual unblock.\n\n\n- Path-linking vs version-pinning a hatch dep doesn't change resolver behaviour.\n- `import \"./X\" for Y` vs bare `import \"./X\"` are equivalent for triggering top-level execution.\n- Bash heredoc / printf escaping for embedding Wren source into wrapper files is a minefield. Use Rust harnesses for proof-of-concept work.\n- Don't trust the canonical name in error/log output — print the requested AND canonical names side by side when debugging import paths.","files":null}]}